 "skipped": [
  {
   "part": "TLC6983ZXLR (and TLC6983RRFR)",
   "why": "no device: the CCSI (no chip select, in-band START/END, 17-bit words with a check bit, dual-edge sampling, chained devices with an index) is not a converter-engine bus style, and the display memory (two banks, auto-advancing write counters, VSYNC bank swap, 48 columns, 16-bit grays, scan period from SEG_LENGTH/LINE_SWT/FREQ_MUL) is beyond the matrix driver. Spec is complete in planning/plan-56-data/specs/is31fl3707-tlc6983.md; the BGA footprint NFBGA-96-ZXL is drawn under missingFootprints (only needed once the device exists; footprint_check fails on an unused footprint, so do not add it before then). RRFR (VQFN-76, p.64) has no footprint drawn and per the task only ZXLR is in stock."
  }
 ],
 "engineNeeds": [
  {
   "need": "matrix driver: a dot is lit while its own field is nonzero",
   "keys": "display.cells: an array of rows, each an array of field names, one per column pin (rows x columns field names, e.g. [[\"PWM_SW1_CS1\", ..., \"PWM_SW1_CS8\"], ...]); a dot is lit while its field is nonzero (optionally display.cellThreshold, lit while the field is >= the threshold). Used instead of `ram`; columnBits then does not apply. Pin SW9/CS8 etc. unchanged.",
   "why": "IS31FL3707 lights a dot when its 8-bit PWM byte is nonzero (p.17: PWM 0 is dark). The matrix driver takes one bit per column from one field per row, and the PWM bytes arrive by the I2C pointer with auto-increment, where no `set` can turn 'byte != 0' into a bit. Today DOTS1-DOTS8 (internal registers, never written) feed `ram`, so every dot is dark. With the feature the device would replace `ram` by `cells` over the 71 PWM registers (PWM_SWx_CSy; SW9 has CS1-CS7) and drop DOTS1-DOTS8. The bench would then add: write PWM bytes (single and auto-increment) and check SWx high with CSy low in the slot of the row."
  },
  {
   "need": "matrix driver: a pin that is a row or a column by a register field, and a row count from a field",
   "keys": "rows / columns entries as {\"pin\": \"SW9/CS8\", \"when\": \"CONFIG.SWS=0\"} (row while the condition holds, else column or idle) and display.rowCount {\"field\": \"CONFIG.SWS\", \"values\": {\"0\": 9, \"1\": 8, \"2\": 7, \"3\": 6, \"4\": 5, \"5\": 4, \"6\": 3, \"7\": 2, \"8\": 9}} (a scan round has that many slots; a value not listed uses the first). SWS 1000 is 'SW1-SW9 with the same phase, all on' (not a scan); 'others' = 9 rows.",
   "why": "IS31FL3707 pin 18 is SW9 in the 7x9 modes and CS8 in the 8x8 modes, and SWS sets 2-9 scanned rows (p.13, p.16). Today the pin is CS8 only and the round is always 8 rows of 29.93 us."
  },
  {
   "need": "shutdown as an OR of conditions",
   "keys": "display.shutdown accepting {\"any\": [\"CONFIG.SSD=0\", \"HW.SDB=0\"]} next to the string / array (AND) forms",
   "why": "IS31FL3707 is dark (switches off) in software shutdown (SSD = 0) OR hardware shutdown (SDB low). `shutdown` arrays are ANDs (all must hold), so only SSD=0 is shutdown and SDB low is mapped to `blank` (columns high, rows still scan). Real chip: rows stop too."
  },
  {
   "need": "CCSI bus style (TLC6983)",
   "keys": "bus {\"style\": \"ccsi\", \"sclk\": \"SCLK\", \"sin\": \"SIN\", \"sout\": \"SOUT\"}: bits sampled on both SCLK edges; idle = SIN high; a frame starts when SIN falls and ends after 18 consecutive high bits; the stream into `protocol` is the 16-bit words with the 17th (check, = NOT of bit 16) bit removed (a check bit that is not the complement could be ignored or drop the frame); read frames insert the device's 3 x 17 bits before the END; chain: a chip-index command (head AA10h) gives the device an index and the device forwards a changed command, SOUT passes SIN delayed by the unselected frames; broadcast commands (all devices act) and non-broadcast data writes (a device removes the last 51 bits of the frame as its own).",
   "why": "tlc6983 p.24-28. The converter engine has no style without a chip select or with in-band framing; this is the TLC6983's only host interface. The single-device case (chain of 1: chip index 0) is enough for a first model."
  },
  {
